IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `poa-dates`

-- Powers of Attorney Act 1998 (Qld) -- the claims a principal, their estate or a beneficiary brings about an attorney's
-- dealings, and the attorney's answers to them; seat-neutral core.
--
-- In the Act's own actors and terms: "the principal" (the person who made the document or appointed the attorney),
-- "the attorney", "the court" (the Supreme Court, schedule 3), "the tribunal" (QCAT, schedule 3), "a third person",
-- "a beneficiary", "the personal representative". The claimant's questions are in poa-seat.l4 and the attorney's in
-- poa-seat-attorney.l4; this module holds the rules.
--
-- Edition: Powers of Attorney Act 1998 (act-1998-022), the version in force from 28 April 2026, current on 6 November
-- 2026, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/act-1998-022.txt. The Act sets its own numbers (6 months, 200 and
-- 300 penalty units); the Succession Act 1981 ss 41(8) and 44, which ss 61D(6) and 107(4) apply, are worked by hand from
-- ../../../succession-act-1981/registers/source-bundle/act-1981-069.txt.
--
-- BASELINE. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Powers of Attorney Act 1998 (Qld)`

§§ `Kinds of attorney (ss 28, 63, 65, 106(9), schedule 3)`

-- schedule 3: "attorney means-- (a) an attorney under a power of attorney, enduring power of attorney or advance health
-- directive; or (b) a statutory health attorney." s 28: an enduring document is an enduring power of attorney or an
-- advance health directive.
DECLARE `Kind of attorney` IS ONE OF
    `an attorney under a general power of attorney made under this Act`
    `an attorney under an enduring power of attorney`
    `an attorney under an advance health directive`
    `an attorney under a power of attorney made otherwise than under this Act`
    `a statutory health attorney`

-- ss 65, 98(1), 99(4), 106(9), 107(5): "an attorney under-- (a) a general power of attorney made under this Act; or (b)
-- an enduring document; or (c) a power of attorney made otherwise than under this Act". Everyone but a statutory health
-- attorney.
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is an attorney under a document` `the attorney` IF
    CONSIDER `the attorney`
    WHEN `a statutory health attorney` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is an attorney under an enduring power of attorney` `the attorney` IF
    CONSIDER `the attorney`
    WHEN `an attorney under an enduring power of attorney` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 28: enduring document.
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is an attorney under an enduring document` `the attorney` IF
    CONSIDER `the attorney`
    WHEN `an attorney under an enduring power of attorney` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an attorney under an advance health directive` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `Capacity (schedule 3, s 41)`

-- schedule 3: "capacity, for a person for a matter, means the person is capable of-- (a) understanding the nature and
-- effect of decisions about the matter; and (b) freely and voluntarily making decisions about the matter; and (c)
-- communicating the decisions in some way." "impaired capacity ... means the person does not have capacity".
GIVEN `understands decisions` IS A BOOLEAN
      `decides freely` IS A BOOLEAN
      `communicates decisions` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `has capacity for the matter` `understands decisions` `decides freely` `communicates decisions` IF
        `understands decisions`
    AND `decides freely`
    AND `communicates decisions`

-- s 41(1): "A principal has capacity to make an enduring power of attorney only if the principal-- (a) is capable of
-- making the enduring power of attorney freely and voluntarily; and (b) understands the nature and effect of the
-- enduring power of attorney." (2) lists what understanding the nature and effect includes; it is asked as one fact.
GIVEN `capable of making it freely and voluntarily` IS A BOOLEAN
      `understood its nature and effect` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `had capacity to make the enduring power of attorney` `capable of making it freely and voluntarily` `understood its nature and effect` IF
        `capable of making it freely and voluntarily`
    AND `understood its nature and effect`

§§ `s 33 -- when an attorney's power under an enduring power of attorney is exercisable`

DECLARE `Kind of matter` IS ONE OF
    `a financial matter`
    `a personal matter`

DECLARE `Exercisable answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: no time was specified, so power for financial matters became exercisable once the enduring power of attorney was made (s 33(2))`
    `yes: the time, circumstance or occasion the enduring power of attorney specifies has come (s 33(1))`
    `yes: the principal has impaired capacity for the matter, and the power is exercisable during that period (s 33(3))`
    `yes: power for a personal matter is exercisable while the principal has impaired capacity for it (s 33(4))`
    `no: power for a personal matter is exercisable only while the principal has impaired capacity for it (s 33(4))`
    `the Act does not make the power exercisable yet: the specified time, circumstance or occasion has not come and the principal has capacity (s 33(1))`

-- s 33(1)-(4). (4) "and not otherwise" closes the personal-matter case: a no. (1) lets the principal specify a time;
-- before it, with capacity, the Act states no answer in its own words (a scope limit without "only": the fork line in
-- NOTES.md).
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `Kind of matter`
      `a time is specified` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the specified time has come` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the principal has impaired capacity` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Exercisable answer`
`the s 33 answer` `the matter` `a time is specified` `the specified time has come` `the principal has impaired capacity` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the matter`
    WHEN `a personal matter` THEN
        IF `the principal has impaired capacity`
        THEN `yes: power for a personal matter is exercisable while the principal has impaired capacity for it (s 33(4))`
        ELSE `no: power for a personal matter is exercisable only while the principal has impaired capacity for it (s 33(4))`
    WHEN `a financial matter` THEN
        IF NOT `a time is specified`
        THEN `yes: no time was specified, so power for financial matters became exercisable once the enduring power of attorney was made (s 33(2))`
        ELSE IF `the specified time has come`
             THEN `yes: the time, circumstance or occasion the enduring power of attorney specifies has come (s 33(1))`
             ELSE IF `the principal has impaired capacity`
                  THEN `yes: the principal has impaired capacity for the matter, and the power is exercisable during that period (s 33(3))`
                  ELSE `the Act does not make the power exercisable yet: the specified time, circumstance or occasion has not come and the principal has capacity (s 33(1))`

§§ `ss 41, 113-114 -- declaring a power of attorney invalid`

DECLARE `Validity answer` IS ONE OF
    `the court may declare it invalid if satisfied the principal did not have the capacity necessary to make it (s 113(2)(a)); if it does, it is void from the start (s 114) and the court may appoint attorneys (s 113(3))`
    `the court may declare it invalid if satisfied it does not comply with the Act's other requirements (s 113(2)(b)); if it does, it is void from the start (s 114) and the court may appoint attorneys (s 113(3))`
    `the court may declare it invalid if satisfied it is invalid for another reason, such as dishonesty or undue influence (s 113(2)(c)); if it does, it is void from the start (s 114) and the court may appoint attorneys (s 113(3))`
    `no: s 113(2) gives no ground for declaring it invalid on these facts`

-- s 113(2): "The court may declare a document ... invalid if the court is satisfied-- (a) the principal did not have the
-- capacity necessary to make it; or (b) it does not comply with the other requirements of this Act; or (c) it is invalid
-- for another reason". The grounds are tried in the text's order. For an enduring power of attorney the capacity is
-- s 41's; for another document it is asked as one fact. s 111A: the court presumes capacity until the contrary is
-- proven, so the capacity facts are what the claimant expects to prove.
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
      `capable of making it freely and voluntarily` IS A BOOLEAN
      `understood its nature and effect` IS A BOOLEAN
      `had the capacity needed for another document` IS A BOOLEAN
      `complies with the Act's other requirements` IS A BOOLEAN
      `invalid for another reason` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Validity answer`
`the s 113 answer` `the attorney` `capable of making it freely and voluntarily` `understood its nature and effect` `had the capacity needed for another document` `complies with the Act's other requirements` `invalid for another reason` MEANS
    IF NOT `had the capacity`
    THEN `the court may declare it invalid if satisfied the principal did not have the capacity necessary to make it (s 113(2)(a)); if it does, it is void from the start (s 114) and the court may appoint attorneys (s 113(3))`
    ELSE IF NOT `complies with the Act's other requirements`
         THEN `the court may declare it invalid if satisfied it does not comply with the Act's other requirements (s 113(2)(b)); if it does, it is void from the start (s 114) and the court may appoint attorneys (s 113(3))`
         ELSE IF `invalid for another reason`
              THEN `the court may declare it invalid if satisfied it is invalid for another reason, such as dishonesty or undue influence (s 113(2)(c)); if it does, it is void from the start (s 114) and the court may appoint attorneys (s 113(3))`
              ELSE `no: s 113(2) gives no ground for declaring it invalid on these facts`
    WHERE
        `had the capacity` MEANS
            IF `is an attorney under an enduring power of attorney` `the attorney`
            THEN `had capacity to make the enduring power of attorney` `capable of making it freely and voluntarily` `understood its nature and effect`
            ELSE `had the capacity needed for another document`

§§ `ss 96, 98 -- acting under an invalid power`

-- s 96: "invalidity ... in relation to a power under a document, means invalidity because-- (i) the document was made in
-- another jurisdiction in purported compliance with the requirements of the law of that other jurisdiction but does not
-- comply with that jurisdiction's requirements; or (ii) the document has been revoked wholly or to the extent it gives
-- the power; or (iii) the power is not exercisable at the time it is purportedly exercised."
DECLARE `Invalidity` IS ONE OF
    `the document was made in another jurisdiction and does not comply with that jurisdiction's requirements`
    `the document has been revoked, wholly or to the extent it gives the power`
    `the power was not exercisable when it was used`
    `none: the power was valid when it was used`

GIVEN `the invalidity` IS AN `Invalidity`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the power was invalid` `the invalidity` IF
    CONSIDER `the invalidity`
    WHEN `none: the power was valid when it was used` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 96: "know ... in relation to the invalidity of a power under a document, includes-- (i) know of the happening of an
-- event that invalidates the power; or (ii) have reason to believe the power is invalid."
GIVEN `knew it was invalid` IS A BOOLEAN
      `knew of an invalidating event` IS A BOOLEAN
      `had reason to believe it was invalid` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `knew of the invalidity` `knew it was invalid` `knew of an invalidating event` `had reason to believe it was invalid` IF
        `knew it was invalid`
    OR  `knew of an invalidating event`
    OR  `had reason to believe it was invalid`

DECLARE `Invalid power answer` IS ONE OF
    `s 98 does not arise: the power was valid when it was used`
    `no: an attorney who used the power without knowing it was invalid incurs no liability, to the principal or anyone else, because of the invalidity (s 98(2)); liability for anything else is not affected`
    `s 98 gives the attorney no protection on these facts: the attorney knew of the invalidity, knew of an event that invalidated the power, or had reason to believe it was invalid`
    `the Act does not settle this: s 98 does not reach a statutory health attorney (s 98(1))`

-- s 98(1)-(2). "without knowing a power is invalid": when the attorney knew, s 98 gives no defence (the ruling of
-- 2026-11-05 on defences: "this section gives no defence on these facts").
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
      `the invalidity` IS AN `Invalidity`
      `the attorney knew` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Invalid power answer`
`the s 98 answer` `the attorney` `the invalidity` `the attorney knew` MEANS
    IF NOT `is an attorney under a document` `the attorney`
    THEN `the Act does not settle this: s 98 does not reach a statutory health attorney (s 98(1))`
    ELSE IF NOT `the power was invalid` `the invalidity`
         THEN `s 98 does not arise: the power was valid when it was used`
         ELSE IF `the attorney knew`
              THEN `s 98 gives the attorney no protection on these facts: the attorney knew of the invalidity, knew of an event that invalidated the power, or had reason to believe it was invalid`
              ELSE `no: an attorney who used the power without knowing it was invalid incurs no liability, to the principal or anyone else, because of the invalidity (s 98(2)); liability for anything else is not affected`

§§ `s 99 -- transactions with a third person under an invalid power`

DECLARE `Third person answer` IS ONE OF
    `s 99(2) does not arise: the power was valid when it was used`
    `the transaction is, in favour of the third person, as valid as if the power were not invalid (s 99(2))`
    `no: s 99(2) does not protect a third person who knew of the invalidity`

-- s 99(2): "A transaction between-- (a) an attorney purporting to use a power that is invalid; and (b) someone else (the
-- third person) who does not know of the invalidity; is, in favour of the third person, as valid as if the power were
-- not invalid." "know" includes having reason to believe (s 96).
GIVEN `the invalidity` IS AN `Invalidity`
      `the third person knew` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Third person answer`
`the s 99(2) answer` `the invalidity` `the third person knew` MEANS
    IF NOT `the power was invalid` `the invalidity`
    THEN `s 99(2) does not arise: the power was valid when it was used`
    ELSE IF `the third person knew`
         THEN `no: s 99(2) does not protect a third person who knew of the invalidity`
         ELSE `the transaction is, in favour of the third person, as valid as if the power were not invalid (s 99(2))`

DECLARE `Purchaser presumption answer` IS ONE OF
    `it is conclusively presumed, in favour of the purchaser, that the third person did not know of the invalidity: they made a statutory declaration before or within 3 months after the purchase was completed (s 99(3)(a))`
    `it is conclusively presumed, in favour of the purchaser, that the third person did not know of the invalidity: the transaction was completed within 1 year after the power of attorney was made (s 99(3)(b))`
    `no: s 99(3) raises no conclusive presumption on these facts; whether the third person knew is a question of fact`

-- s 99(3): "it is conclusively presumed in favour of the purchaser that the third person did not at the material time
-- know of the invalidity of the attorney's power if-- (a) the third person makes a statutory declaration before or
-- within 3 months after the completion of the purchase ...; or (b) the transaction between the attorney and the third
-- person was completed within 1 year after the power of attorney was made." Readings: "within 3 months after" ends on
-- the same day 3 months later; "within 1 year after" on the same day a year later (poa-dates.l4).
GIVEN `a declaration was made` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the declaration` IS A DATE
      `the day the purchase was completed` IS A DATE
      `the day the transaction was completed` IS A DATE
      `the day the power of attorney was made` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Purchaser presumption answer`
`the s 99(3) answer` `a declaration was made` `the day of the declaration` `the day the purchase was completed` `the day the transaction was completed` `the day the power of attorney was made` MEANS
    IF      `a declaration was made`
        AND `the day of the declaration` `is no later than` (`the day` 3 `months after` `the day the purchase was completed`)
    THEN `it is conclusively presumed, in favour of the purchaser, that the third person did not know of the invalidity: they made a statutory declaration before or within 3 months after the purchase was completed (s 99(3)(a))`
    ELSE IF `the day the transaction was completed` `is no later than` (`the day` 12 `months after` `the day the power of attorney was made`)
         THEN `it is conclusively presumed, in favour of the purchaser, that the third person did not know of the invalidity: the transaction was completed within 1 year after the power of attorney was made (s 99(3)(b))`
         ELSE `no: s 99(3) raises no conclusive presumption on these facts; whether the third person knew is a question of fact`

§§ `s 73 -- conflict transactions`

-- s 73(6): "A conflict transaction is a transaction in which there may be conflict, or which results in conflict,
-- between-- (a) the duty of an attorney towards the principal; and (b) either-- (i) the interests of the attorney, or a
-- relation, business associate or close friend of the attorney; or (ii) another duty of the attorney."
DECLARE `Conflict` IS ONE OF
    `the attorney's own interests`
    `the interests of a relation, business associate or close friend of the attorney`
    `another duty of the attorney`
    `nothing: there is no possible conflict with the attorney's duty to the principal`

-- s 73(7)-(8), and example 6 to s 73(6).
DECLARE `Source of the conflict` IS ONE OF
    `merely that the attorney is related to the principal`
    `merely that the attorney may be a beneficiary of the principal's estate`
    `merely that the attorney, in their own right and for the principal, deals with or acquires a joint interest in property, or borrows or guarantees for that`
    `the transaction is a gift or donation made under s 88`
    `the transaction provides for a dependant of the principal under s 89`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Authorisation` IS ONE OF
    `the principal authorised it, its type, or conflict transactions generally, beforehand`
    `the court authorised it beforehand under s 118(2)`
    `the principal, having capacity, authorised it afterwards (s 73(2))`
    `the court authorised it afterwards (s 118(3))`
    `nobody has authorised it`

DECLARE `Conflict answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: it is not a conflict transaction (s 73(6))`
    `no: a transaction is not a conflict transaction merely because of that (s 73(7))`
    `no: a gift or donation under s 88 is not a conflict transaction (s 73(8))`
    `no: providing for a dependant under s 89 is not a conflict transaction (s 73(6), example 6)`
    `yes, a conflict transaction, and the attorney was authorised to enter into it (s 73(1))`
    `yes, a conflict transaction authorised afterwards: it is taken to be, and always to have been, as valid as if authorised beforehand (s 73(3) or s 118(4))`
    `yes, a conflict transaction entered into without authorisation: the attorney has acted contrary to s 73(1) until it is authorised (s 73(5)); the court may still relieve the attorney (s 105)`

-- s 73(1)-(8). "may enter into a conflict transaction only if the principal, or the court under section 118(2), has
-- authorised" it; (2)-(3) and s 118(3)-(4) authorise it afterwards; (5) "until the conflict transaction is authorised
-- ..., the attorney has acted contrary to subsection (1)". Example 6 is read as the text's own statement (fork line in
-- NOTES.md).
GIVEN `the conflict` IS A `Conflict`
      `the source` IS A `Source of the conflict`
      `the authorisation` IS AN `Authorisation`
GIVETH A `Conflict answer`
`the s 73 answer` `the conflict` `the source` `the authorisation` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the conflict`
    WHEN `nothing: there is no possible conflict with the attorney's duty to the principal` THEN `no: it is not a conflict transaction (s 73(6))`
    OTHERWISE
        CONSIDER `the source`
        WHEN `merely that the attorney is related to the principal` THEN `no: a transaction is not a conflict transaction merely because of that (s 73(7))`
        WHEN `merely that the attorney may be a beneficiary of the principal's estate` THEN `no: a transaction is not a conflict transaction merely because of that (s 73(7))`
        WHEN `merely that the attorney, in their own right and for the principal, deals with or acquires a joint interest in property, or borrows or guarantees for that` THEN `no: a transaction is not a conflict transaction merely because of that (s 73(7))`
        WHEN `the transaction is a gift or donation made under s 88` THEN `no: a gift or donation under s 88 is not a conflict transaction (s 73(8))`
        WHEN `the transaction provides for a dependant of the principal under s 89` THEN `no: providing for a dependant under s 89 is not a conflict transaction (s 73(6), example 6)`
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `the authorisation`
            WHEN `the principal authorised it, its type, or conflict transactions generally, beforehand` THEN `yes, a conflict transaction, and the attorney was authorised to enter into it (s 73(1))`
            WHEN `the court authorised it beforehand under s 118(2)` THEN `yes, a conflict transaction, and the attorney was authorised to enter into it (s 73(1))`
            WHEN `the principal, having capacity, authorised it afterwards (s 73(2))` THEN `yes, a conflict transaction authorised afterwards: it is taken to be, and always to have been, as valid as if authorised beforehand (s 73(3) or s 118(4))`
            WHEN `the court authorised it afterwards (s 118(3))` THEN `yes, a conflict transaction authorised afterwards: it is taken to be, and always to have been, as valid as if authorised beforehand (s 73(3) or s 118(4))`
            OTHERWISE `yes, a conflict transaction entered into without authorisation: the attorney has acted contrary to s 73(1) until it is authorised (s 73(5)); the court may still relieve the attorney (s 105)`

DECLARE `Other party answer` IS ONE OF
    `the transaction is, in favour of the other party, as valid as if it were not a conflict transaction (s 73(9))`
    `s 73(9) does not protect the other party: they knew, or had reason to believe, it was a conflict transaction`

-- s 73(9): "A conflict transaction between an attorney and a person who does not know, or have reason to believe, the
-- transaction is a conflict transaction is, in favour of the person, as valid as if the transaction were not a conflict
-- transaction." A defence-like protection: when the person knew, s 73(9) gives none.
GIVEN `the other party knew` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the other party had reason to believe` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Other party answer`
`the s 73(9) answer` `the other party knew` `the other party had reason to believe` MEANS
    IF `the other party knew` OR `the other party had reason to believe`
    THEN `s 73(9) does not protect the other party: they knew, or had reason to believe, it was a conflict transaction`
    ELSE `the transaction is, in favour of the other party, as valid as if it were not a conflict transaction (s 73(9))`

§§ `s 87 -- presumption of undue influence`

-- schedule 3: "relation, of a person, means-- (a) a spouse ...; (b) a person who is related to the first person by
-- blood, marriage or adoption or because of a de facto relationship, foster relationship or a relationship arising
-- because of a legal arrangement; (c) a person on whom the first person is completely or mainly dependent; (d) a person
-- who is completely or mainly dependent on the first person; (e) a person who is a member of the same household". "close
-- friend, of a person, means another person who has a close personal relationship with the first person and a personal
-- interest in the first person's welfare." "business associate" is not defined: asked as a fact.
DECLARE `Other party` IS ONE OF
    `the attorney`
    `the attorney's spouse`
    `a person related to the attorney by blood, marriage, adoption, a de facto or foster relationship, or a legal arrangement`
    `a person on whom the attorney is completely or mainly dependent`
    `a person who is completely or mainly dependent on the attorney`
    `a member of the attorney's household`
    `a business associate of the attorney`
    `a person with a close personal relationship with the attorney and a personal interest in the attorney's welfare`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Undue influence answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the transaction gives rise to a presumption in your favour that you were induced to enter it by the attorney's undue influence (s 87)`
    `the Act does not settle this: s 87 raises the presumption only for an attorney under an enduring power of attorney or advance health directive, or their relation, business associate or close friend; the general law may still`

-- s 87: "The fact that a transaction is between a principal and 1 or more of the following-- (a) an attorney under an
-- enduring power of attorney or advance health directive; (b) a relation, business associate or close friend of the
-- attorney; gives rise to a presumption in the principal's favour ...". The list is not closed with "only", so outside
-- it the answer is "not settled" (the ruling of 2026-11-04).
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
      `the other party` IS AN `Other party`
GIVETH AN `Undue influence answer`
`the s 87 answer` `the attorney` `the other party` MEANS
    IF      `is an attorney under an enduring document` `the attorney`
        AND `the other party is the attorney or close to them`
    THEN `yes: the transaction gives rise to a presumption in your favour that you were induced to enter it by the attorney's undue influence (s 87)`
    ELSE `the Act does not settle this: s 87 raises the presumption only for an attorney under an enduring power of attorney or advance health directive, or their relation, business associate or close friend; the general law may still`
    WHERE
        `the other party is the attorney or close to them` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the other party`
            WHEN `someone else` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `Part 3 -- financial matters under an enduring power of attorney (ss 83-89)`

DECLARE `Investment answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is an authorised investment (s 84(1))`
    `yes: the attorney may continue an investment the principal had when the power became exercisable (s 84(2))`
    `no: an attorney for financial matters may invest only in authorised investments (s 84(1))`
    `no: Part 3 applies only to enduring powers of attorney (s 83)`

-- s 83: "Except where otherwise provided, this part applies only to enduring powers of attorney." s 84(1): "An attorney
-- for financial matters may invest only in authorised investments." (2) continuing investments. (3) "authorised
-- investment means-- (a) an investment which, if the investment were of trust funds by a trustee, would be an investment
-- in accordance with the Trusts Act 2025; or (b) an investment approved by the tribunal." The Trusts Act is not
-- deposited: (a) is asked as a fact.
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
      `a trustee could make it under the Trusts Act 2025` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the tribunal approved it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the principal had it when the power became exercisable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Investment answer`
`the s 84 answer` `the attorney` `a trustee could make it under the Trusts Act 2025` `the tribunal approved it` `the principal had it when the power became exercisable` MEANS
    IF NOT `is an attorney under an enduring power of attorney` `the attorney`
    THEN `no: Part 3 applies only to enduring powers of attorney (s 83)`
    ELSE IF `a trustee could make it under the Trusts Act 2025` OR `the tribunal approved it`
         THEN `yes: it is an authorised investment (s 84(1))`
         ELSE IF `the principal had it when the power became exercisable`
              THEN `yes: the attorney may continue an investment the principal had when the power became exercisable (s 84(2))`
              ELSE `no: an attorney for financial matters may invest only in authorised investments (s 84(1))`

DECLARE `Separate property answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the attorney must keep their property separate from the principal's (s 86(1)); not doing so is an offence, up to 300 penalty units`
    `no: s 86(1) does not apply to property the principal and the attorney own jointly (s 86(2)(a))`
    `no: s 86(1) does not apply to property they acquired jointly in place of jointly owned property (s 86(2)(b))`
    `no: Part 3 applies only to enduring powers of attorney (s 83)`

-- s 86: "(1) An attorney for a financial matter must keep the attorney's property separate from the principal's
-- property. Maximum penalty--300 penalty units. (2) Subsection (1) does not apply to-- (a) property owned jointly by the
-- principal and attorney; or (b) property acquired jointly by the principal and attorney in place of property owned
-- jointly by the principal and attorney." (3) other obligations are not affected.
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
      `the property is owned jointly` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it was acquired jointly in place of jointly owned property` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Separate property answer`
`the s 86 answer` `the attorney` `the property is owned jointly` `it was acquired jointly in place of jointly owned property` MEANS
    IF NOT `is an attorney under an enduring power of attorney` `the attorney`
    THEN `no: Part 3 applies only to enduring powers of attorney (s 83)`
    ELSE IF `the property is owned jointly`
         THEN `no: s 86(1) does not apply to property the principal and the attorney own jointly (s 86(2)(a))`
         ELSE IF `it was acquired jointly in place of jointly owned property`
              THEN `no: s 86(1) does not apply to property they acquired jointly in place of jointly owned property (s 86(2)(b))`
              ELSE `yes: the attorney must keep their property separate from the principal's (s 86(1)); not doing so is an offence, up to 300 penalty units`

DECLARE `Gift answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the gift is otherwise authorised under this Act (s 88(1))`
    `yes: the gift meets s 88(1); the attorney, or a charity connected with them, may receive it (s 88(2))`
    `no: the gift is not of a nature the principal made when they had capacity, or might reasonably be expected to make (s 88(1)(a))`
    `no: the value is more than is reasonable given all the circumstances, in particular the principal's financial circumstances (s 88(1)(b))`
    `no: Part 3 applies only to enduring powers of attorney (s 83)`

-- s 88(1): "Unless otherwise authorised under this Act, an attorney for a principal may give away or donate the
-- principal's property only if-- (a) the gift or donation is-- (i) of the nature the principal made when the principal
-- had capacity; or (ii) of the nature the principal might reasonably be expected to make; and (b) the value ... is not
-- more than what is reasonable having regard to all the circumstances and, in particular, the principal's financial
-- circumstances." Whether the value is reasonable is the court's or tribunal's judgment: an input.
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
      `otherwise authorised under this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `of a kind the principal made with capacity` IS A BOOLEAN
      `of a kind the principal might reasonably be expected to make` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the value is reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Gift answer`
`the s 88 answer` `the attorney` `otherwise authorised under this Act` `of a kind the principal made with capacity` `of a kind the principal might reasonably be expected to make` `the value is reasonable` MEANS
    IF NOT `is an attorney under an enduring power of attorney` `the attorney`
    THEN `no: Part 3 applies only to enduring powers of attorney (s 83)`
    ELSE IF `otherwise authorised under this Act`
         THEN `yes: the gift is otherwise authorised under this Act (s 88(1))`
         ELSE IF NOT (`of a kind the principal made with capacity` OR `of a kind the principal might reasonably be expected to make`)
              THEN `no: the gift is not of a nature the principal made when they had capacity, or might reasonably be expected to make (s 88(1)(a))`
              ELSE IF NOT `the value is reasonable`
                   THEN `no: the value is more than is reasonable given all the circumstances, in particular the principal's financial circumstances (s 88(1)(b))`
                   ELSE `yes: the gift meets s 88(1); the attorney, or a charity connected with them, may receive it (s 88(2))`

DECLARE `Dependant answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the attorney may provide for the dependant's needs from the principal's estate, up to what is reasonable (s 89)`
    `yes: the enduring power of attorney expresses a contrary intention, so the reasonableness limit in s 89(2) does not apply`
    `no: what is provided must not be more than is reasonable given all the circumstances, in particular the principal's financial circumstances (s 89(2))`
    `the Act does not settle this: s 89 is for a dependant, a person completely or mainly dependent on the principal`
    `no: Part 3 applies only to enduring powers of attorney (s 83)`

-- s 89: "(1) An attorney for financial matters for an individual may provide from the principal's estate for the needs
-- of a dependant of the principal. (2) However, unless there is a contrary intention expressed in the enduring power of
-- attorney, what is provided must not be more than what is reasonable ...". schedule 3: "dependant, of a principal,
-- means a person who is completely or mainly dependent on the principal." A person outside the grant: "not settled"
-- (a grant limited in its own words, the ruling of 2026-11-04).
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
      `the person is completely or mainly dependent on the principal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the enduring power of attorney expresses a contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the amount is reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Dependant answer`
`the s 89 answer` `the attorney` `the person is completely or mainly dependent on the principal` `the enduring power of attorney expresses a contrary intention` `the amount is reasonable` MEANS
    IF NOT `is an attorney under an enduring power of attorney` `the attorney`
    THEN `no: Part 3 applies only to enduring powers of attorney (s 83)`
    ELSE IF NOT `the person is completely or mainly dependent on the principal`
         THEN `the Act does not settle this: s 89 is for a dependant, a person completely or mainly dependent on the principal`
         ELSE IF `the enduring power of attorney expresses a contrary intention`
              THEN `yes: the enduring power of attorney expresses a contrary intention, so the reasonableness limit in s 89(2) does not apply`
              ELSE IF `the amount is reasonable`
                   THEN `yes: the attorney may provide for the dependant's needs from the principal's estate, up to what is reasonable (s 89)`
                   ELSE `no: what is provided must not be more than is reasonable given all the circumstances, in particular the principal's financial circumstances (s 89(2))`

§§ `Offences (ss 26, 61, 66, 71, 74A, 86)`

DECLARE `Conduct` IS ONE OF
    `dishonestly inducing a person to make or revoke a power of attorney (s 26)`
    `dishonestly inducing a person to make or revoke an enduring document (s 61)`
    `not exercising power honestly and with reasonable diligence to protect the principal's interests (s 66(1))`
    `exercising a power the attorney knows has been revoked (s 71)`
    `using confidential information gained as attorney other than as s 74 allows, without a reasonable excuse (s 74A)`
    `not keeping the attorney's property separate from the principal's (s 86(1))`
    `other conduct`

-- The maximum penalties the sections state, in penalty units. The dollar value of a penalty unit is set outside the
-- Act (Penalties and Sentences Act 1992 and its regulation, not deposited): answers are in penalty units. Other conduct:
-- the Act states no penalty for it in these sections (0, and the seat answer says so).
GIVEN `the conduct` IS A `Conduct`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum penalty in penalty units` `the conduct` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the conduct`
    WHEN `not keeping the attorney's property separate from the principal's (s 86(1))` THEN 300
    WHEN `other conduct` THEN 0
    OTHERWISE 200

§§ `ss 66, 106 -- compensation and accounting for profits`

DECLARE `Payee` IS ONE OF
    `the principal`
    `the principal's estate`

DECLARE `Compensation answer` IS ONE OF
    `the court or tribunal may order the attorney to pay compensation for the loss or to account for the profits, but not both for the same exercise of power (s 106(1)-(2)), paid to` HAS `the payee` IS A `Payee`
    `the court or tribunal may order the attorney to pay compensation for the loss (s 106(1)(a)), paid to` HAS `the payee` IS A `Payee`
    `the court or tribunal may order the attorney to account for the profits (s 106(1)(b)), paid to` HAS `the payee` IS A `Payee`
    `no: s 106 does not reach a statutory health attorney (s 106(9))`
    `s 106 gives no order on these facts: it needs a failure to comply with this Act in the exercise of a power`
    `s 106 gives no order on these facts: it needs a loss caused by the failure, or profits the attorney accrued from it`

-- s 106(1): "The court or tribunal may order an attorney for a principal to pay an amount to the principal or, if the
-- principal has died, the principal's estate-- (a) to compensate for a loss caused by the attorney's failure to comply
-- with this Act in the exercise of a power; or (b) to account for any profits the attorney has accrued as a result of
-- the attorney's failure ...". (2) not both for the same exercise of power. (3) even if convicted; (4) even if the
-- appointment has ended. (9) "attorney means" a document attorney: a statutory health attorney is outside the
-- definition. "court means any court" (s 106(9)). s 66(2) gives the same compensation for a failure to act honestly and
-- with reasonable diligence; s 106 covers it.
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
      `the attorney failed to comply with the Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the failure caused a loss` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the attorney accrued profits from it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the principal has died` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Compensation answer`
`the s 106 answer` `the attorney` `the attorney failed to comply with the Act` `the failure caused a loss` `the attorney accrued profits from it` `the principal has died` MEANS
    IF NOT `is an attorney under a document` `the attorney`
    THEN `no: s 106 does not reach a statutory health attorney (s 106(9))`
    ELSE IF NOT `the attorney failed to comply with the Act`
         THEN `s 106 gives no order on these facts: it needs a failure to comply with this Act in the exercise of a power`
         ELSE IF `the failure caused a loss` AND `the attorney accrued profits from it`
              THEN `the court or tribunal may order the attorney to pay compensation for the loss or to account for the profits, but not both for the same exercise of power (s 106(1)-(2)), paid to` `the payee`
              ELSE IF `the failure caused a loss`
                   THEN `the court or tribunal may order the attorney to pay compensation for the loss (s 106(1)(a)), paid to` `the payee`
                   ELSE IF `the attorney accrued profits from it`
                        THEN `the court or tribunal may order the attorney to account for the profits (s 106(1)(b)), paid to` `the payee`
                        ELSE `s 106 gives no order on these facts: it needs a loss caused by the failure, or profits the attorney accrued from it`
    WHERE
        `the payee` MEANS IF `the principal has died` THEN `the principal's estate` ELSE `the principal`

DECLARE `Application time answer` IS ONE OF
    `s 106 sets no application time while the principal and the attorney are both alive`
    `apply within 6 months after the death (s 106(5)); the court or tribunal may extend the time (s 106(7)); the last day is` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE
    `apply within 6 months after the first death (s 106(6)); the court or tribunal may extend the time (s 106(7)); the last day is` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE

-- s 106(5)-(7): "(5) If the principal or attorney has died, an application for an order under subsection (1) must be
-- made to the court or tribunal within 6 months after the death. (6) If the principal and attorney have died, an
-- application ... must be made ... within 6 months after the first death. (7) The court or tribunal may extend the
-- application time."
GIVEN `the principal has died` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day the principal died` IS A DATE
      `the attorney has died` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day the attorney died` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Application time answer`
`the s 106 time answer` `the principal has died` `the day the principal died` `the attorney has died` `the day the attorney died` MEANS
    IF `the principal has died` AND `the attorney has died`
    THEN `apply within 6 months after the first death (s 106(6)); the court or tribunal may extend the time (s 106(7)); the last day is` (`the day` 6 `months after` (`the earlier of` `the day the principal died` `and` `the day the attorney died`))
    ELSE IF `the principal has died`
         THEN `apply within 6 months after the death (s 106(5)); the court or tribunal may extend the time (s 106(7)); the last day is` (`the day` 6 `months after` `the day the principal died`)
         ELSE IF `the attorney has died`
              THEN `apply within 6 months after the death (s 106(5)); the court or tribunal may extend the time (s 106(7)); the last day is` (`the day` 6 `months after` `the day the attorney died`)
              ELSE `s 106 sets no application time while the principal and the attorney are both alive`

§§ `s 107 -- compensation for a benefit in the estate lost by the attorney's dealing`

DECLARE `Lost benefit applicant` IS ONE OF
    `the person whose benefit was lost (even if that person is the attorney)`
    `the personal representative of the person whose benefit was lost`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Lost benefit answer` IS ONE OF
    `you may apply to the Supreme Court for compensation out of the principal's estate, which must not exceed the value of the lost benefit (s 107(2)-(3)); at most` HAS `the most the court can order` IS A NUMBER
    `no: s 107 does not reach a statutory health attorney (s 107(5))`
    `no: s 107 applies only if a benefit in the principal's estate, under the will, on intestacy or by another disposition taking effect on death, is lost because of a sale or other dealing with the principal's property by an attorney`
    `the Act does not give you a s 107 application: s 107(2) names the person whose benefit was lost, or their personal representative`

-- s 107(1): "This section applies if a person's benefit in a principal's estate under the principal's will, on
-- intestacy, or by another disposition taking effect on the principal's death, is lost because of a sale or other
-- dealing with the principal's property by an attorney of the principal." (1A) even if the person is the attorney. (2)
-- "The person, or the person's personal representative, may apply to the Supreme Court for compensation out of the
-- principal's estate." (3) "... the compensation must not exceed the value of the lost benefit."
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
      `the benefit was lost because of the attorney's dealing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the applicant` IS A `Lost benefit applicant`
      `the value of the lost benefit` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Lost benefit answer`
`the s 107 answer` `the attorney` `the benefit was lost because of the attorney's dealing` `the applicant` `the value of the lost benefit` MEANS
    IF NOT `is an attorney under a document` `the attorney`
    THEN `no: s 107 does not reach a statutory health attorney (s 107(5))`
    ELSE IF NOT `the benefit was lost because of the attorney's dealing`
         THEN `no: s 107 applies only if a benefit in the principal's estate, under the will, on intestacy or by another disposition taking effect on death, is lost because of a sale or other dealing with the principal's property by an attorney`
         ELSE CONSIDER `the applicant`
              WHEN `someone else` THEN `the Act does not give you a s 107 application: s 107(2) names the person whose benefit was lost, or their personal representative`
              OTHERWISE `you may apply to the Supreme Court for compensation out of the principal's estate, which must not exceed the value of the lost benefit (s 107(2)-(3)); at most` `the value of the lost benefit`

§§ `ss 61A-61D -- a beneficiary's interest in the proceeds of property the attorney dealt with`

DECLARE `Proceeds answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you have the same interest in the proceeds as you would have had in the property, and you are entitled to income traced from them and any capital gain on them (s 61B(2)-(3)); this holds even if you are the attorney (s 61B(4))`
    `yes, but subject to the court's order under s 61D(1) (s 61B(5)): you have the same interest in the proceeds as you would have had in the property, with traced income and capital gains (s 61B(2)-(3))`
    `no: ss 61B to 61D apply only to an enduring power of attorney (s 61A)`
    `the Act does not settle this under s 61B: it is for a beneficiary under a deceased principal's will, of property an attorney dealt with under an enduring power of attorney (see s 107 for a lost benefit)`

-- s 61A: "Sections 61B to 61D apply only in relation to an enduring power of attorney." s 61B(1): "This section applies
-- to a person who is a beneficiary ... under a deceased principal's will." (2) the same interest in the surplus money
-- or other property (the proceeds) from a sale, mortgage, charge, disposition or other dealing with property under the
-- powers given to an attorney under an enduring power of attorney; (3) traced income and capital gain; (4) even if the
-- beneficiary is the attorney; (5) subject to a s 61D(1) order.
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
      `I am a beneficiary under the deceased principal's will` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the attorney dealt with the property under the enduring power of attorney` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court has made a s 61D order` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Proceeds answer`
`the s 61B answer` `the attorney` `I am a beneficiary under the deceased principal's will` `the attorney dealt with the property under the enduring power of attorney` `the court has made a s 61D order` MEANS
    IF NOT `is an attorney under an enduring power of attorney` `the attorney`
    THEN `no: ss 61B to 61D apply only to an enduring power of attorney (s 61A)`
    ELSE IF NOT (`I am a beneficiary under the deceased principal's will` AND `the attorney dealt with the property under the enduring power of attorney`)
         THEN `the Act does not settle this under s 61B: it is for a beneficiary under a deceased principal's will, of property an attorney dealt with under an enduring power of attorney (see s 107 for a lost benefit)`
         ELSE IF `the court has made a s 61D order`
              THEN `yes, but subject to the court's order under s 61D(1) (s 61B(5)): you have the same interest in the proceeds as you would have had in the property, with traced income and capital gains (s 61B(2)-(3))`
              ELSE `yes: you have the same interest in the proceeds as you would have had in the property, and you are entitled to income traced from them and any capital gain on them (s 61B(2)-(3)); this holds even if you are the attorney (s 61B(4))`

DECLARE `S 61D applicant` IS ONE OF
    `a beneficiary under the principal's will`
    `the personal representative of a deceased beneficiary under the principal's will`
    `the personal representative of the principal`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `S 61D answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may apply to the Supreme Court (not QCAT, s 61D(7)) for an order giving effect to s 61B, or preventing an unjust and disproportionate advantage or disadvantage the will did not contemplate (s 61D(1)-(2)); apply within 6 months after the principal's death (s 61D(4)), which the court may extend (s 61D(5))`
    `no: ss 61B to 61D apply only to an enduring power of attorney (s 61A)`
    `the Act does not give you a s 61D application: s 61D(2) names a beneficiary under the will, a deceased beneficiary's personal representative, and the principal's personal representative`

-- s 61D(1)-(2), (4)-(5), (7).
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
      `the applicant` IS A `S 61D applicant`
GIVETH A `S 61D answer`
`the s 61D answer` `the attorney` `the applicant` MEANS
    IF NOT `is an attorney under an enduring power of attorney` `the attorney`
    THEN `no: ss 61B to 61D apply only to an enduring power of attorney (s 61A)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the applicant`
         WHEN `someone else` THEN `the Act does not give you a s 61D application: s 61D(2) names a beneficiary under the will, a deceased beneficiary's personal representative, and the principal's personal representative`
         OTHERWISE `yes: you may apply to the Supreme Court (not QCAT, s 61D(7)) for an order giving effect to s 61B, or preventing an unjust and disproportionate advantage or disadvantage the will did not contemplate (s 61D(1)-(2)); apply within 6 months after the principal's death (s 61D(4)), which the court may extend (s 61D(5))`

§§ `Application times as regulative rules (ss 61D(4), 106(5)-(6), 107(4), 124)`

DECLARE `POA party` IS ONE OF
    `the applicant`
    `the court`

DECLARE `POA act` IS ONE OF
    `apply for an order under s 106`
    `apply for an order under s 61D`
    `apply for compensation under s 107`
    `give written reasons for the decision`

-- s 106(5)-(6). Day 0: the death (the first death, if both have died). The days allowed run to the same day 6 months
-- later. Missing it is no offence: the application is out of time unless the court or tribunal extends it (s 106(7)).
GIVEN `days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `POA party` `POA act`
DECIDE `s 106 -- the applicant must apply within 6 months after the death` `days allowed` IS
    PARTY `the applicant`
    MUST  `apply for an order under s 106`
    WITHIN `days allowed`
    LEST  BREACH BY `the applicant` BECAUSE "s 106(5)-(6): the application was not made within 6 months after the death; it can proceed only if the court or tribunal extends the application time (s 106(7))"

-- s 61D(4). Day 0: the principal's death.
GIVEN `days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `POA party` `POA act`
DECIDE `s 61D -- the applicant must apply within 6 months after the principal's death` `days allowed` IS
    PARTY `the applicant`
    MUST  `apply for an order under s 61D`
    WITHIN `days allowed`
    LEST  BREACH BY `the applicant` BECAUSE "s 61D(4): the application was not made within 6 months after the principal's death; it can proceed only if the court extends the application time (s 61D(5))"

-- s 107(4) applies the Succession Act 1981 s 41(8): "Unless the court otherwise directs, no application shall be heard by
-- the court ... unless the proceedings for such application be instituted within 9 months after the death of the
-- deceased". Day 0: the principal's death.
GIVEN `days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `POA party` `POA act`
DECIDE `s 107 -- the applicant must apply within 9 months after the principal's death` `days allowed` IS
    PARTY `the applicant`
    MUST  `apply for compensation under s 107`
    WITHIN `days allowed`
    LEST  BREACH BY `the applicant` BECAUSE "s 107(4) with Succession Act 1981 s 41(8): the proceedings were not started within 9 months after the principal's death; the application is heard only if the court otherwise directs"

-- s 124: "On application by a person the court considers has a sufficient interest in obtaining reasons for its
-- decision, the court must give written reasons for the decision within 28 days after the application." Day 0: the
-- application for reasons. The court's duty is encoded for what the person must be given, and by when.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `POA party` `POA act`
DECIDE `s 124 -- the court must give written reasons within 28 days` IS
    PARTY `the court`
    MUST  `give written reasons for the decision`
    WITHIN 28
    LEST  BREACH BY `the court` BECAUSE "s 124: written reasons were not given within 28 days after the application for them"

DECLARE `Application timing` IS ONE OF
    `in time; the last day is` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE
    `out of time, unless the time is extended or the court directs otherwise; the last day was` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE

-- Runs a must-apply rule from the day of the death to the day of the application, and reads the trace: FULFILLED is in
-- time; anything else (the breach) is out of time.
GIVEN `the rule` IS A FUNCTION FROM NUMBER TO DEONTIC `POA party` `POA act`
      `the application` IS A `POA act`
      `the death` IS A DATE
      `the last day` IS A DATE
      `the day of the application` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Application timing`
`the timing of` `the rule` `the application` `the death` `the last day` `the day of the application` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the rule` (`the days from` `the death` `to` `the last day`)) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `the application` (`the days from` `the death` `to` `the day of the application`)))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN `in time; the last day is` `the last day`
    OTHERWISE `out of time, unless the time is extended or the court directs otherwise; the last day was` `the last day`

-- s 106: the death the 6 months run from is the first death if both have died (s 106(6)).
GIVEN `the day the 6 months run from` IS A DATE
      `the day of the application` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Application timing`
`the s 106 timing` `the day the 6 months run from` `the day of the application` MEANS
    `the timing of` `s 106 -- the applicant must apply within 6 months after the death` `apply for an order under s 106` `the day the 6 months run from` (`the day` 6 `months after` `the day the 6 months run from`) `the day of the application`

GIVEN `the day the principal died` IS A DATE
      `the day of the application` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Application timing`
`the s 61D timing` `the day the principal died` `the day of the application` MEANS
    `the timing of` `s 61D -- the applicant must apply within 6 months after the principal's death` `apply for an order under s 61D` `the day the principal died` (`the day` 6 `months after` `the day the principal died`) `the day of the application`

GIVEN `the day the principal died` IS A DATE
      `the day of the application` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Application timing`
`the s 107 timing` `the day the principal died` `the day of the application` MEANS
    `the timing of` `s 107 -- the applicant must apply within 9 months after the principal's death` `apply for compensation under s 107` `the day the principal died` (`the day` 9 `months after` `the day the principal died`) `the day of the application`

§§ `ss 61D(6), 107(4) with Succession Act 1981 s 44 -- the personal representative's distribution`

DECLARE `Distribution answer` IS ONE OF
    `protected: no action lies against the personal representative for the distribution, because you notified them in writing that you consent to it or do not intend to apply (Succession Act s 44(2))`
    `protected: no action lies against the personal representative for a distribution properly made at least 6 months after the death without notice of an application (Succession Act s 44(3)(a))`
    `protected: no action lies against the personal representative for a distribution properly made at least 9 months after the death, with no written notice that the application had been started and no service of it (Succession Act s 44(3)(b))`
    `no: Succession Act s 44(2)-(3) does not protect this distribution`

-- Succession Act 1981 s 44, applied by ss 61D(6) and 107(4): "(2) No person who may have made or may be entitled to make an
-- application ... shall be entitled to bring an action against the personal representative by reason of the personal
-- representative having distributed any part of the estate if the distribution was properly made ... after the person
-- (being of full legal capacity) has notified the personal representative in writing that the person either-- (a)
-- consents to the distribution; or (b) does not intend to make any application ...". "(3) No action shall lie against the
-- personal representative ... if the distribution was properly made ...-- (a) not earlier than 6 months after the
-- deceased's death and without notice of any application or intended application ...; or (b) if notice ... has been
-- received--not earlier than 9 months after the deceased's death, unless the personal representative receives written
-- notice that the application has been commenced in the court or is served with a copy of the application." (4) notice
-- is in writing signed by the applicant or their solicitor. s 44(1) (distributions for a dependent spouse or child) is
-- out of scope. Whether a distribution was "properly made" is a judgment: an input. "if" conditions: a no.
GIVEN `the day the principal died` IS A DATE
      `the day of the distribution` IS A DATE
      `it was properly made` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I consented or said I would not apply, in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I gave written notice of my application before the distribution` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the personal representative had written notice the application had started, or was served` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Distribution answer`
`the s 44 answer` `the day the principal died` `the day of the distribution` `it was properly made` `I consented or said I would not apply, in writing` `I gave written notice of my application before the distribution` `the personal representative had written notice the application had started, or was served` MEANS
    IF NOT `it was properly made`
    THEN `no: Succession Act s 44(2)-(3) does not protect this distribution`
    ELSE IF `I consented or said I would not apply, in writing`
         THEN `protected: no action lies against the personal representative for the distribution, because you notified them in writing that you consent to it or do not intend to apply (Succession Act s 44(2))`
         ELSE IF NOT `I gave written notice of my application before the distribution`
              THEN IF (`the day` 6 `months after` `the day the principal died`) `is no later than` `the day of the distribution`
                   THEN `protected: no action lies against the personal representative for a distribution properly made at least 6 months after the death without notice of an application (Succession Act s 44(3)(a))`
                   ELSE `no: Succession Act s 44(2)-(3) does not protect this distribution`
              ELSE IF     (`the day` 9 `months after` `the day the principal died`) `is no later than` `the day of the distribution`
                      AND (NOT `the personal representative had written notice the application had started, or was served`)
                   THEN `protected: no action lies against the personal representative for a distribution properly made at least 9 months after the death, with no written notice that the application had been started and no service of it (Succession Act s 44(3)(b))`
                   ELSE `no: Succession Act s 44(2)-(3) does not protect this distribution`

§§ `ss 109A, 110 -- who may apply, and where`

DECLARE `Applicant` IS ONE OF
    `the principal`
    `the principal's spouse`
    `the principal's child aged 18 or more (including a stepchild, adopted child, or a person the principal fostered or was guardian of as a child)`
    `the principal's parent (including a step-parent, adoptive parent, foster-parent or guardian)`
    `the principal's sibling aged 18 or more (including a step-sibling, adopted sibling or foster-sibling)`
    `an attorney of the principal`
    `a health provider of the principal`
    `another person with a sufficient and genuine concern for the principal's rights and interests`
    `another person`

DECLARE `Standing answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes, as the principal (s 110(2), (3)(a))`
    `yes, as a member of the principal's family (s 110(3)(b), (5))`
    `yes, as an attorney (s 110(3)(c))`
    `yes, as a health provider, because the application is about an advance health directive or a health matter (s 110(3)(e))`
    `yes, as an interested person: someone with a sufficient and genuine concern for the principal's rights and interests (s 110(2), (3)(f))`
    `the Act does not settle this: s 110 does not name you, so you may apply only as an interested person (someone with a sufficient and genuine concern for the principal's rights and interests)`

-- s 110(2)-(3), (5); schedule 3 "interested person". A sibling is family only "if there is no person mentioned in
-- paragraph (a), (b) or (c) who is reasonably available" (s 110(5)(d)). The list is not closed with "only" and (f) admits
-- any interested person: outside it, "not settled".
GIVEN `the applicant` IS AN `Applicant`
      `a spouse, adult child or parent is reasonably available` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the application is about an advance health directive or a health matter` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Standing answer`
`the s 110 answer` `the applicant` `a spouse, adult child or parent is reasonably available` `the application is about an advance health directive or a health matter` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the applicant`
    WHEN `the principal` THEN `yes, as the principal (s 110(2), (3)(a))`
    WHEN `the principal's spouse` THEN `yes, as a member of the principal's family (s 110(3)(b), (5))`
    WHEN `the principal's child aged 18 or more (including a stepchild, adopted child, or a person the principal fostered or was guardian of as a child)` THEN `yes, as a member of the principal's family (s 110(3)(b), (5))`
    WHEN `the principal's parent (including a step-parent, adoptive parent, foster-parent or guardian)` THEN `yes, as a member of the principal's family (s 110(3)(b), (5))`
    WHEN `the principal's sibling aged 18 or more (including a step-sibling, adopted sibling or foster-sibling)` THEN
        IF `a spouse, adult child or parent is reasonably available`
        THEN `the Act does not settle this: s 110 does not name you, so you may apply only as an interested person (someone with a sufficient and genuine concern for the principal's rights and interests)`
        ELSE `yes, as a member of the principal's family (s 110(3)(b), (5))`
    WHEN `an attorney of the principal` THEN `yes, as an attorney (s 110(3)(c))`
    WHEN `a health provider of the principal` THEN
        IF `the application is about an advance health directive or a health matter`
        THEN `yes, as a health provider, because the application is about an advance health directive or a health matter (s 110(3)(e))`
        ELSE `the Act does not settle this: s 110 does not name you, so you may apply only as an interested person (someone with a sufficient and genuine concern for the principal's rights and interests)`
    WHEN `another person with a sufficient and genuine concern for the principal's rights and interests` THEN `yes, as an interested person: someone with a sufficient and genuine concern for the principal's rights and interests (s 110(2), (3)(f))`
    OTHERWISE `the Act does not settle this: s 110 does not name you, so you may apply only as an interested person (someone with a sufficient and genuine concern for the principal's rights and interests)`

DECLARE `Application kind` IS ONE OF
    `compensation or an account of profits under s 106`
    `compensation for a lost benefit under s 107`
    `an order under s 61D about a beneficiary's interest in proceeds`
    `an order for records, accounts or an audit under s 122`
    `another declaration, order, direction, recommendation or advice under chapter 6`

DECLARE `Forum answer` IS ONE OF
    `any court, or QCAT (s 106(1), (9))`
    `the Supreme Court (s 107(2))`
    `the Supreme Court; not QCAT (s 61D(7))`
    `the Supreme Court or QCAT (s 122(1))`
    `the Supreme Court, or QCAT, because the document is an enduring document (ss 109A, 110)`
    `the Supreme Court (s 110); QCAT has the court's jurisdiction only for enduring documents (s 109A)`

-- s 106(1), (9) "court means any court"; s 107(2) "the Supreme Court"; s 61D(7) "Despite section 109A, a reference in
-- this section to the court does not include a reference to the tribunal"; s 122(1) "the court or the tribunal"; s
-- 109A(1) "The tribunal is given the same jurisdiction and powers for enduring documents as the Supreme Court."
GIVEN `the application kind` IS AN `Application kind`
      `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
GIVETH A `Forum answer`
`the forum answer` `the application kind` `the attorney` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the application kind`
    WHEN `compensation or an account of profits under s 106` THEN `any court, or QCAT (s 106(1), (9))`
    WHEN `compensation for a lost benefit under s 107` THEN `the Supreme Court (s 107(2))`
    WHEN `an order under s 61D about a beneficiary's interest in proceeds` THEN `the Supreme Court; not QCAT (s 61D(7))`
    WHEN `an order for records, accounts or an audit under s 122` THEN `the Supreme Court or QCAT (s 122(1))`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `is an attorney under an enduring document` `the attorney`
        THEN `the Supreme Court, or QCAT, because the document is an enduring document (ss 109A, 110)`
        ELSE `the Supreme Court (s 110); QCAT has the court's jurisdiction only for enduring documents (s 109A)`

§§ `s 122 -- records and audit`

DECLARE `Records answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court or QCAT may order the attorney to file and serve on you a summary of receipts and expenditure, or more detailed accounts, for a stated period; have the accounts audited; or present a plan of management, even if the enduring power of attorney has been revoked or the principal has died (s 122)`
    `the Act does not settle this: s 122 is for an attorney for a financial matter under an enduring power of attorney`
    `the Act does not settle this: s 122(2) names an application by the principal or another interested person (the court or QCAT may also act on its own initiative)`

-- s 122(1)-(2), (4).
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
      `the attorney is attorney for a financial matter` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I am the principal or an interested person` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Records answer`
`the s 122 answer` `the attorney` `the attorney is attorney for a financial matter` `I am the principal or an interested person` MEANS
    IF NOT (`is an attorney under an enduring power of attorney` `the attorney` AND `the attorney is attorney for a financial matter`)
    THEN `the Act does not settle this: s 122 is for an attorney for a financial matter under an enduring power of attorney`
    ELSE IF `I am the principal or an interested person`
         THEN `yes: the court or QCAT may order the attorney to file and serve on you a summary of receipts and expenditure, or more detailed accounts, for a stated period; have the accounts audited; or present a plan of management, even if the enduring power of attorney has been revoked or the principal has died (s 122)`
         ELSE `the Act does not settle this: s 122(2) names an application by the principal or another interested person (the court or QCAT may also act on its own initiative)`

§§ `ss 123, 125 -- costs`

DECLARE `Costs answer` IS ONE OF
    `the court may order you to pay another participant's costs, and direct that you not make another application of that type without leave (s 123(2))`
    `the court's order about costs governs (s 125)`
    `costs follow the event: as the successful party you can expect your costs to be paid by the unsuccessful party (s 125(2))`
    `costs follow the event: as the unsuccessful party you can expect to pay the successful party's costs (s 125(2))`

-- s 123(1)-(2): dismissal of a frivolous, trivial, vexatious, misconceived or insubstantial application, with costs and a
-- leave direction "if the court considers it appropriate". s 125: "(1) The costs of a proceeding are within the court's
-- discretion. (2) However, unless the court otherwise orders, costs follow the event."
GIVEN `my application was dismissed under s 123` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court made another order about costs` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I succeeded` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Costs answer`
`the costs answer` `my application was dismissed under s 123` `the court made another order about costs` `I succeeded` MEANS
    IF `my application was dismissed under s 123`
    THEN `the court may order you to pay another participant's costs, and direct that you not make another application of that type without leave (s 123(2))`
    ELSE IF `the court made another order about costs`
         THEN `the court's order about costs governs (s 125)`
         ELSE IF `I succeeded`
              THEN `costs follow the event: as the successful party you can expect your costs to be paid by the unsuccessful party (s 125(2))`
              ELSE `costs follow the event: as the unsuccessful party you can expect to pay the successful party's costs (s 125(2))`

§§ `s 124 -- written reasons`

DECLARE `Reasons answer` IS ONE OF
    `the court must give you written reasons by` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE
    `no: s 124 obliges the court only on application by a person it considers has a sufficient interest in obtaining reasons`

GIVEN `the day I applied for reasons` IS A DATE
      `the court considers I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Reasons answer`
`the s 124 answer` `the day I applied for reasons` `the court considers I have a sufficient interest` MEANS
    IF `the court considers I have a sufficient interest`
    THEN `the court must give you written reasons by` (`the day` 28 `days after` `the day I applied for reasons`)
    ELSE `no: s 124 obliges the court only on application by a person it considers has a sufficient interest in obtaining reasons`

§§ `Answering a claim: ss 72, 82, 97, 105`

DECLARE `Court advice answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: an attorney who acts in compliance with the court's advice, directions or recommendations is taken to have complied with this Act (s 97)`
    `no: s 97 does not protect an attorney who knowingly gave the court false or misleading information relevant to its advice`
    `the Act does not settle this under s 97: it protects an attorney acting in compliance with the court's advice, directions or recommendations`

-- s 97: "An attorney who acts in compliance with the court's advice, directions or recommendations is taken to have
-- complied with this Act unless the attorney knowingly gave the court false or misleading information relevant to the
-- court's advice, directions or recommendations."
GIVEN `I acted in compliance with the court's advice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I knowingly gave the court false or misleading information` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Court advice answer`
`the s 97 answer` `I acted in compliance with the court's advice` `I knowingly gave the court false or misleading information` MEANS
    IF NOT `I acted in compliance with the court's advice`
    THEN `the Act does not settle this under s 97: it protects an attorney acting in compliance with the court's advice, directions or recommendations`
    ELSE IF `I knowingly gave the court false or misleading information`
         THEN `no: s 97 does not protect an attorney who knowingly gave the court false or misleading information relevant to its advice`
         ELSE `yes: an attorney who acts in compliance with the court's advice, directions or recommendations is taken to have complied with this Act (s 97)`

DECLARE `Relief answer` IS ONE OF
    `the court may relieve you from all or part of your personal liability for the breach (s 105(1))`
    `no: s 105 gives no relief on these facts; the court must consider you acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused`
    `s 105 does not arise: you are not, and may not be, personally liable for a breach of this Act`

-- s 105(1): "If the court considers-- (a) an attorney is, or may be, personally liable for a breach of this Act; and (b)
-- the attorney has acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused for the breach; the court may relieve
-- the attorney from all or part of the attorney's personal liability". (2) every kind of attorney, including a statutory
-- health attorney. The court's view is an input.
GIVEN `I am or may be personally liable for a breach` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court considers I acted honestly and reasonably` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court considers I ought fairly to be excused` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Relief answer`
`the s 105 answer` `I am or may be personally liable for a breach` `the court considers I acted honestly and reasonably` `the court considers I ought fairly to be excused` MEANS
    IF NOT `I am or may be personally liable for a breach`
    THEN `s 105 does not arise: you are not, and may not be, personally liable for a breach of this Act`
    ELSE IF `the court considers I acted honestly and reasonably` AND `the court considers I ought fairly to be excused`
         THEN `the court may relieve you from all or part of your personal liability for the breach (s 105(1))`
         ELSE `no: s 105 gives no relief on these facts; the court must consider you acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused`

DECLARE `Resignation answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may resign for the matter by signed notice given to the principal (s 72(1)); another way the law allows also remains (s 72(2))`
    `only with the court's leave: while the principal has impaired capacity for the matter, an attorney under an enduring document may resign only with the court's leave (s 82(1)); the court may appoint a replacement (s 82(2))`
    `yes: the court has given leave, so you may resign for the matter (s 82(1)); the court may appoint a replacement (s 82(2))`
    `the Act does not settle this: ss 72 and 82 do not reach a statutory health attorney`

-- s 72(1): "An attorney may resign as attorney for a matter by signed notice given to the principal." s 82(1): "Despite
-- section 72, while a principal has impaired capacity for a matter, an attorney under an enduring document may only
-- resign as attorney for the matter with the court's leave." Part 1 (s 65) does not apply to a statutory health
-- attorney; s 82 (Part 2) does, but speaks of "an attorney under an enduring document": outside both, not settled.
GIVEN `the attorney` IS A `Kind of attorney`
      `the principal has impaired capacity for the matter` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court has given leave` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Resignation answer`
`the resignation answer` `the attorney` `the principal has impaired capacity for the matter` `the court has given leave` MEANS
    IF NOT `is an attorney under a document` `the attorney`
    THEN `the Act does not settle this: ss 72 and 82 do not reach a statutory health attorney`
    ELSE IF `is an attorney under an enduring document` `the attorney` AND `the principal has impaired capacity for the matter`
         THEN IF `the court has given leave`
              THEN `yes: the court has given leave, so you may resign for the matter (s 82(1)); the court may appoint a replacement (s 82(2))`
              ELSE `only with the court's leave: while the principal has impaired capacity for the matter, an attorney under an enduring document may resign only with the court's leave (s 82(1)); the court may appoint a replacement (s 82(2))`
         ELSE `yes: you may resign for the matter by signed notice given to the principal (s 72(1)); another way the law allows also remains (s 72(2))`
